## Why This Is Asked
Tests understanding of graph reachability and fixed-point semantics in logical implications, with practical applications in policy and permission systems.

## Key Concepts
- Upward-closure in partially ordered sets
- Fixed-point computation via forward-closure
- Linear-time graph traversal algorithms
- Cycle handling through visited state management

## Code Example
```javascript
function propagate(T, edges) {
  const trueSet = new Set(T);
  const queue = [...T];
  const adjacency = new Map();

  for (const [a, b] of edges) {
    if (!adjacency.has(a)) adjacency.set(a, []);
    adjacency.get(a).push(b);
  }

  while (queue.length > 0) {
    const x = queue.shift();
    for (const y of adjacency.get(x) || []) {
      if (!trueSet.has(y)) {
        trueSet.add(y);
        queue.push(y);
      }
    }
  }

  return trueSet;
}
```

## Example
Consider flags {A,B,C,D,E,F} with relations: A→B, B→C, C→A (cycle), A→D, D→E, E→F, B→F. If T={A}, propagation yields {A,B,C,D,E,F} as the least model containing T.

## Why This Is Asked

Tests ability to reason about constraints in practical feature-flag settings and to design a solver using propagation and backtracking rather than pure search. It also exposes how to craft a constructive counterexample when needed.

## Key Concepts

- Constraint satisfaction with implications and disjunctions
- Unit propagation, backtracking, and a decision trail
- Real-world constraints in deployment feature flags and release gates

## Code Example

```javascript
// Minimal backtracker for boolean flags with given constraints
function sat(S){
  return (!S.S1 || S.S2) && (!S.S2 || S.S3) && (!S.S4 || !S.S1) && (S.S1 || S.S4) && (S.S2 || S.S4);
}
function satPartial(part){
  const v = (k)=> part.hasOwnProperty(k)? part[k] : null;
  if (v('S1')===true && v('S2')===false) return false;
  if (v('S2')===true && v('S3')===false) return false;
  if (v('S4')===true && v('S1')===true) return false;
  if (v('S1')===false && v('S4')===false) return false;
  if (v('S2')===false && v('S4')===false) return false;
  return true;
}
function solve(partial={}, idx=0){
  const vars=['S1','S2','S3','S4'];
  if (!satPartial(partial)) return null;
  if (idx===vars.length) return partial;
  const name = vars[idx];
  for (const val of [false,true]){
    const next = Object.assign({}, partial, {[name]: val});
    const res = solve(next, idx+1);
    if (res) return res;
  }
  return null;
}
console.log(solve());
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you scale this to thousands of flags with mostly Horn clauses?
- What optimizations would you add for large constraint sets?

Why This Is Asked
Assess the ability to reason with Kleene 3-valued semantics in a constrained CSP and to design a propagation/backtracking approach that handles unknowns.

Key Concepts
- Kleene 3-valued logic: T>U>F; ¬T=F, ¬F=T, ¬U=U; ∧/∨ use min/max under T>U>F; A→B := ¬A ∨ B.
- Constraint propagation in a small CSP with unknowns; existence of a model containing U.
- Backtracking strategies to prune non-viable branches.

Code Example
```javascript
const V = {T:'T', F:'F', U:'U'}
functionNot(x){ if (x==='T') return 'F'; if (x==='F') return 'T'; return 'U' }
function kleeneOr(a,b){ if (a==='T' || b==='T') return 'T'; if (a==='U' || b==='U') return 'U'; return 'F' }
function kleeneAnd(a,b){ if (a==='F' || b==='F') return 'F'; if (a==='U' || b==='U') return 'U'; return 'T' }
function implies(a,b){ return kleeneOr(functionNot(a), b) }
```

## Why This Is Asked
Tests ability to design and reason about constrained, multi-valued logic with propagation and backtracking, mirroring solver internals.

## Key Concepts
- Three-valued logic propagation (T, F, U)
- Horn-like implications
- Backtracking with forward-propagation and early pruning

## Code Example
```javascript
// sketch: propagate(rule): if antecedent is T, set consequent to T; if consequent is F, fail
function propagate(state) {
  let changed = false;
  for (const r of rules) {
    if (r.antecedent(state) === true) {
      if (state[r.consequent] !== 'T') { state[r.consequent] = 'T'; changed = true; }
    }
  }
  return changed;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you extend to n variables and cycles while guaranteeing termination?
- How would you implement heuristics to choose which U to branch on first to minimize backtracks?

